PG.notes("l322", "<p class=\"summary\">Opens the Web3 module (made with the DFINITY Foundation). Frames Web3 as the third stage of the web, explains why blockchain development is a career opportunity, and previews example projects built on blockchains.</p>\n<h3>Why the module exists</h3>\n<ul><li>The module is a collaboration with the DFINITY Foundation, the organisation behind the Internet Computer, and teaches building applications on a blockchain rather than trading cryptocurrency.</li><li>Motivation given: a survey poll of learners placed Web3/blockchain top of the list of things people want to learn, and a job-market statistic cited growth in blockchain developer openings far above machine-learning openings, with high junior salaries in the US. (Extra) Treat salary and job-growth figures as marketing-era claims from early 2022 and check current data before relying on them.</li><li>The course stance: as a developer you build the technology rather than speculate on coin prices, so investment advice is explicitly out of scope.</li></ul>\n<h3>The three stages of the web</h3>\n<ul><li>Web 1.0 (1990s): mostly read-only pages. Visitors consumed information that publishers posted (static portals and directories).</li><li>Web 2.0: interactive, read-and-write. Users create the content on platforms such as social networks and video/forum sites, so the platform companies own the data and the network.</li><li>Web 3.0: from about 2018, websites and applications are built on a blockchain instead of on a company's private servers. The comparison made is to the early internet boom: heavy investment and a new technology just taking off.</li></ul>\n<h3>Examples of Web3 applications</h3>\n<ul><li>Helium: a community-run wireless network where individuals host hotspots and earn cryptocurrency for providing coverage in remote places.</li><li>PoolTogether: a no-loss lottery. Pooled deposits earn interest, the interest is paid to one winner, and depositors can withdraw their principal at any time.</li><li>Axie Infinity: a creature-collecting game with a tradeable in-game economy. Developers take a small percentage (4.25%) of each trade, so trading volume turns into developer revenue. Worked example: roughly 3 million in daily trades x 0.0425 is about 127,000 per day in fees.</li><li>The takeaway is that applications can embed their own money flows because the blockchain handles ownership and payment.</li></ul>\n<h3>What comes next</h3>\n<ul><li>Before building apps, the next lesson explains how a blockchain works, then DApps and the Internet Computer follow.</li></ul>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Do not read this lecture as financial advice; it explicitly avoids recommending which cryptocurrencies to buy.</li><li>Figures (poll percentages, salaries, trade volumes) are a 2022 snapshot and will have changed.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l323", "<p class=\"summary\">Builds up blockchain from first principles: a ledger, why encryption is not enough, one-way hash functions, blocks mined with a nonce, chaining by previous-block hash, distribution across peers, and finally smart contracts. Uses two interactive hash and blockchain demos linked in the course resources.</p>\n<h3>The ledger and its two problems</h3>\n<ul><li>A blockchain is a ledger: an ordered record of who owes or paid whom and how much (e.g. A owes B 10, A paid C 5).</li><li>Problem 1: every participant must agree on which transactions belong in the ledger.</li><li>Problem 2: whoever holds the ledger must be trusted not to edit past entries. A paper or plain database ledger can be altered (changing 10 to 100), so a way to make history immutable is required.</li></ul>\n<h3>Encryption (reversible) and why it is insufficient</h3>\n<ul><li>Encryption turns a message into a ciphertext that is hard to read, and it can be reversed by anyone who knows the method and key. The Caesar cipher is the simplest example: shift every letter by a fixed number (shift 4 turns E into I).</li><li>In the demo, repeated letters in the plaintext produce repeated letters in the ciphertext, so patterns leak and you can brute force all 26 shifts.</li><li>Because encryption can always be decrypted, a ledger protected by it could still be tampered with by someone who recovers the key or breaks the scheme (the Enigma machine is cited as a broken cipher). (Extra) Encryption protects secrecy; blockchains need tamper evidence, which is a different property.</li></ul>\n<h3>Hashing: a one-way function</h3>\n<ul><li>A hash function takes any input and produces a fixed-length output (the hash/digest). SHA-256 always yields 64 hex characters, whether you hash 'abc' or a whole novel.</li><li>Changing even one character of the input changes the hash completely and unpredictably.</li><li>It is one-way: computing the hash is fast, but recovering the input from the hash is practically impossible except by guessing.</li><li>Intuition via factoring: multiplying 3 x 19 to get 57 is instant, but finding the factors of a large number means trying divisors one by one. Real cryptography uses numbers hundreds of digits long; a 240-digit number took researchers about 900 years of computing time to factor in 2019. (Extra) SHA-256 is not literally factoring; the analogy only conveys easy forwards / infeasible backwards.</li></ul>\n<h3>A block: data + nonce + hash</h3>\n<ul><li>A block holds a piece of data (a transaction). Hashing the block's contents gives a hash that acts as its fingerprint/identifier.</li><li>The chain sets a rule for a valid block, for example the hash must start with a number of zeros (the difficulty). Five leading zeros is harder than three.</li><li>The data should stay clean, so a changeable number called the nonce is added to the hashed content. Mining means trying nonce = 0, 1, 2 ... until the hash meets the rule. It is pure brute force and the demo's Mine button automates it (the video's runs found nonces like 3885 for three zeros and 53,792 for five).</li><li>A nonce only works for that exact data. Edit the data afterwards and the hash no longer meets the rule, so the block shows as invalid (red in the demo).</li></ul>\n<h3>The chain: linking by previous hash</h3>\n<ul><li>Each block's hash is computed from three things: its data, its nonce and the previous block's hash. That linkage is the chain.</li><li>The first block is the Genesis block; it has no predecessor so its previous hash is all zeros (64 of them).</li><li>Editing an old block changes its hash, which changes the input of the next block, invalidating it and every block after. To repair the chain an attacker must re-mine every later block.</li><li>On real networks such as Bitcoin or Ethereum, re-mining is infeasible with current computing power; even the small demo chain can freeze the browser at high difficulty.</li></ul>\n<h3>Distribution: solving agreement</h3>\n<ul><li>In the distributed demo, several peers (A, B, C) each hold a full copy of the chain. Identical data gives identical hashes across peers.</li><li>If one peer alters a block and re-mines their own chain to look valid, their hashes now differ from everyone else's, so the network identifies that peer as dishonest.</li><li>The result is a distributed, trustless ledger: you rely on the protocol and the many copies, not on a single company holding the records (the example contrasts it with trusting a payment provider).</li></ul>\n<h3>Smart contracts</h3>\n<ul><li>Ethereum's extension: blocks can store code as well as data. Stored code is covered by the hash so it cannot be changed afterwards.</li><li>Example: a crowdfunding contract that, when total pledges reach a goal, automatically credits the project owner's account. The outcome is recorded on-chain, replacing an intermediary that holds the escrow.</li><li>Building apps whose logic runs automatically on-chain is the aim of the rest of the module.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Toy blockchain: SHA-256, mining a nonce, chaining and tamper check (own illustrative code)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">// Illustrative toy blockchain (Node.js) - own code, not from the lecture\nconst crypto = require(&quot;crypto&quot;);\nconst sha256 = (s) =&gt; crypto.createHash(&quot;sha256&quot;).update(s).digest(&quot;hex&quot;); // always 64 hex chars\n\nfunction mine(data, prevHash, difficulty) {\n  const target = &quot;0&quot;.repeat(difficulty);\n  let nonce = 0;\n  while (true) {\n    const hash = sha256(`${nonce}${data}${prevHash}`);\n    if (hash.startsWith(target)) return { data, prevHash, nonce, hash };\n    nonce++;                          // brute force: try the next number\n  }\n}\n\nconst genesisPrev = &quot;0&quot;.repeat(64);\nconst b1 = mine(&quot;Angela owes Jack $10&quot;, genesisPrev, 4);\nconst b2 = mine(&quot;Angela gives Jenny $5&quot;, b1.hash, 4);   // chained via b1.hash\n\n// Validity check: recompute hash and confirm the zeros rule\nconst valid = (b, d) =&gt; sha256(`${b.nonce}${b.data}${b.prevHash}`) === b.hash &amp;&amp; b.hash.startsWith(&quot;0&quot;.repeat(d));\nb1.data = &quot;Jack owes Angela $10&quot;;   // tamper\nconsole.log(valid(b1, 4));          // false - and b2.prevHash no longer matches b1's real hash</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Smart contract idea in pseudocode</p><pre data-lang=\"txt\">// Smart contract idea in pseudocode (Extra: Ethereum would use Solidity)\nif (totalPledged &gt;= goal) {\n  balances[owner] += totalPledged;   // runs automatically, code is fixed on-chain\n}</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Confusing hashing with encryption: hashes cannot be reversed, ciphertext can.</li><li>Thinking the nonce is part of the data; it is an extra value tuned until the hash meets the difficulty rule.</li><li>Assuming one peer's edited chain is accepted: other peers' copies disagree with it.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Real Bitcoin mining also hashes the block header, uses double SHA-256 and a dynamic difficulty target; the course demo is simplified.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l324", "<p class=\"summary\">Explains what DApps change about building companies and products (token funding, DAOs, pseudonymity), gives examples, and sets out the problem of building on Ethereum (cost, storage, no real backend) that motivates the Internet Computer.</p>\n<h3>DApps as the backbone of Web3</h3>\n<ul><li>A DApp (decentralised application) runs its logic on a blockchain rather than on servers owned by one company.</li><li>Traditional startup path: build the product, deploy, raise venture funding, maybe go public later. Web3 path: build the product and issue tokens early, so funding can start from day one by selling tokens that grant use of or a stake in the app.</li></ul>\n<h3>DAOs and governance tokens</h3>\n<ul><li>A DAO (Decentralised Autonomous Organisation) replaces the board and org chart: token holders vote on the project's direction.</li><li>Governance tokens give voting rights. Incentives align because good decisions raise the app's value and therefore the token price.</li></ul>\n<h3>Funding comparison used in the lecture</h3>\n<ul><li>Dropbox (centralised file storage) raised a Series A of about 6 million dollars. Filecoin (decentralised file storage spread over many computers, giving redundancy) raised about 257 million dollars in its initial coin offering.</li><li>Point being made: DApps can raise money earlier and sometimes in far larger amounts. (Extra) ICOs carry heavy regulatory and fraud risk; treat the numbers as illustrative, not as a business model recommendation.</li></ul>\n<h3>Possible DApp ideas</h3>\n<ul><li>A rideshare service owned and governed by drivers and riders through tokens.</li><li>Games where players pay an entry stake and winners receive cash prizes automatically.</li><li>General rule: anything benefiting from a trustless, decentralised system where users and creators share the upside.</li></ul>\n<h3>Pseudonymity</h3>\n<ul><li>Participants can be known by a handle without revealing name, age or location, so work is judged on its merit. The lecture cites teenagers who built the DeFi lending project Rari Capital as an example.</li></ul>\n<h3>Choosing a chain and Ethereum's limits</h3>\n<ul><li>Many chains exist (Ethereum, Avalanche, Polkadot, Solana). Ethereum hosts the most DApps simply because it introduced smart contracts first.</li><li>Its scaling problem: computation is expensive and on-chain storage is impractical, so there is effectively no proper backend or database.</li><li>NFTs on Ethereum usually store only a URL; the image lives elsewhere (IPFS/Filecoin or a private cloud server such as AWS). Many DApp front ends (HTML, CSS, JS, images) are also hosted on AWS, so the app is a patchwork of services rather than fully on-chain.</li><li>The Internet Computer, begun by DFINITY in 2014, aims to be a scalable cloud-like computer on a blockchain: fast computation, direct data storage, and full applications hosted on-chain. It is the platform used for the rest of the module.</li></ul>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Assuming an NFT's image is stored on-chain; usually only a link is.</li><li>Assuming a DApp's website is decentralised just because its contract is; front ends are often on centralised hosting.</li><li>Treating token fundraising figures as typical outcomes.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l325", "<p class=\"summary\">Introduces the Internet Computer: a blockchain designed to host an entire application stack (compute, storage, front end) on-chain via canisters. Contrasts it with Ethereum's cost and throughput limits and explains why canister state persistence simplifies development.</p>\n<h3>Goal: run the whole web stack on-chain</h3>\n<ul><li>The Internet Computer (IC) aims to be one secure protocol that covers hosting, storage and services, so apps do not lean on AWS, Azure or browser-extension wallets for the bulk of their logic.</li><li>Current DApps are only partly decentralised: a small slice of logic and data is on-chain while most sits with big Web2 providers, which can shut a project down. (Extra) A DApp with a centralised dependency inherits that provider's censorship and outage risk.</li><li>The lecture notes that Ethereum's founder named the IC a leading competitor in 2019; treat as a quoted claim.</li></ul>\n<h3>Why existing chains cannot do it</h3>\n<ul><li>Ethereum was built mainly as a ledger, not for heavy computation or bulk storage.</li><li>Cost illustration: storing one gigabyte (roughly 300 phone photos) on Ethereum was quoted at hundreds of millions of dollars at the time, and a day's worth of Instagram photo uploads would cost trillions. Figures are 2022-era estimates.</li><li>Throughput: every state change (for example a post in an on-chain social app) is a transaction, and transactions per second (TPS) caps how fast the app can update. Ethereum then produced a block every ~14 seconds at ~15 TPS, so an on-chain Twitter clone would allow only a handful of posts per minute at high fees.</li></ul>\n<h3>How the Internet Computer is built</h3>\n<ul><li>DFINITY researched scalable smart contracts from about 2015 and produced a consensus design called threshold relay, enabling much faster finality than other layer-one chains.</li><li>The IC pools capacity from many independent data centres worldwide using the Internet Computer Protocol, forming one decentralised 'world computer'.</li><li>The unit of deployment is the canister (canister smart contract): it runs code and stores that code's data. Users reach canisters over normal HTTPS requests.</li><li>Each canister holds a WebAssembly module plus its memory pages. Developers write in languages that compile to WebAssembly, such as Rust or DFINITY's own language Motoko (used in this course).</li><li>Canisters behave like sandboxed containers (similar in spirit to Docker) but their program state is preserved indefinitely, as though the process never exits. Variables, arrays and dictionaries just keep their values, so there is no need to write database code for persistence. (Extra) This is called orthogonal persistence; state survives upgrades only when written to be stable.</li></ul>\n<h3>Ecosystem facts mentioned</h3>\n<ul><li>Over 30,000 canisters were running at recording time, with hundreds of applications including decentralised Reddit-style and TikTok-style apps.</li><li>CanCan example: liking a video that later goes viral rewards early likers with tokens, showing how on-chain logic enables token-economy features directly in the app.</li><li>DFINITY grants of 25,000 to 100,000 dollars were advertised for builders. (Extra) Programme terms change; check the current DFINITY grants page.</li></ul>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Believing hosting a front end on AWS is compatible with a fully decentralised DApp.</li><li>Treating canister memory like a free database: upgrades need stable memory handling (Extra).</li><li>Treating quoted costs and counts as current.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l326", "<p class=\"summary\">Short prerequisite notice: the Web3 modules are advanced and assume the earlier course content has been completed.</p>\n<h3>Prerequisites</h3>\n<ul><li>The Web3 material sits at the end of the course because it is harder than the earlier sections.</li><li>Assumed knowledge: React, vanilla JavaScript, and building both front ends and back ends.</li><li>Learners who jumped straight to this section without web development experience should expect difficulty and go back to the earlier parts when confused.</li></ul>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Starting section 37 without React and JavaScript fundamentals.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l327", "<p class=\"summary\">Mac walkthrough for installing Node LTS, the dfx SDK, VS Code with the Motoko extension, then creating, starting, deploying and running a Hello World Internet Computer project locally.</p>\n<h3>Prerequisites and tools</h3>\n<ul><li>The lesson links a published document with every command; the installer command must be copied whole, because dropping the trailing quote breaks it.</li><li>Node.js: use the LTS release, not the newest feature release, because the Internet Computer tooling targets LTS. Check with node --version; the lecture asks for version 16 or above.</li><li>dfx: the DFINITY command-line SDK used to run a local Internet Computer, build and deploy canisters, and execute code. Installed by pasting a one-line install command into Terminal (open via Spotlight). Verify with dfx --version (0.9.3 in the video). (Extra) The current installer and version numbers differ now; always use the up-to-date command from the DFINITY docs.</li><li>VS Code plus the extension named Motoko published by the DFINITY Foundation (several similarly named extensions exist). It gives syntax support for the Motoko language.</li></ul>\n<h3>Project folder and scaffolding</h3>\n<ul><li>Create a single parent folder (ic-projects) in the home directory to hold all Internet Computer projects.</li><li>In Terminal, cd into it (typing cd then dragging the folder in fills the path), then run the dfx new command with a project name. This generates a sample app: Motoko backend code, HTML/JavaScript frontend, and config. The DFINITY logo printed in the terminal signals completion.</li><li>Open the generated folder in VS Code via Open to browse the Motoko, HTML and JS files.</li></ul>\n<h3>Run and deploy locally</h3>\n<ul><li>Terminal 1: dfx start launches the local replica (the local Internet Computer network). Wait for the server-listening info message and leave it running.</li><li>Terminal 2 (split terminal button in VS Code): dfx deploy builds the project and installs its canisters on the local network. It takes a while the first time.</li><li>Then npm start serves the frontend; the printed URL opens the app.</li><li>The sample page has a name input and button that calls the backend greet function and shows a greeting. Seeing it work proves the toolchain is set up.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Mac setup sequence (summary of lesson steps)</p><pre data-lang=\"sh\">node --version          # want 16+ (use the LTS release)\n# install dfx using the one-line installer command from the lesson's Google doc (copy it whole, including closing quotes)\ndfx --version           # lecture shows 0.9.3\nmkdir ~/ic-projects &amp;&amp; cd ~/ic-projects\ndfx new hello_world     # scaffolds the sample project (sample Motoko + frontend)\ncd hello_world\n# terminal 1\ndfx start               # starts the local Internet Computer replica; wait for the 'listening' info line\n# terminal 2\ndfx deploy              # builds and installs canisters on the local network\nnpm start               # dev server; open the URL it prints</pre>\n<h2>The task: Install tools and run the Hello World canister app</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> A Mac with Terminal; VS Code optional until step 4.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Have a local Internet Computer replica running and the sample DApp greeting you in the browser.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Check node --version is 16+ (install the LTS build if not).</li><li>Run the dfx installer command from the lesson document, then confirm with dfx --version.</li><li>Install VS Code and the Motoko extension from the DFINITY Foundation.</li><li>Create ic-projects in your home folder, cd into it, run dfx new with a project name.</li><li>Open the project folder in VS Code.</li><li>In one terminal run dfx start and wait for the listening message; in a second run dfx deploy, then npm start.</li><li>Open the printed URL, enter your name, press the button and confirm the greeting.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> The browser page shows a name box and a button; submitting returns a greeting with your name.</p>\n</div>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Installing Node's current release instead of LTS.</li><li>Truncating the pasted installer command (missing closing quote).</li><li>Closing the dfx start terminal before deploying; it must keep running.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Newer dfx versions scaffold a different layout (hello_backend / hello_frontend with Vite) and may use dfx start --background and different install commands; follow current docs.</li><li>Windows users skip this lesson.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l328", "<p class=\"summary\">Windows walkthrough: verify system requirements, install WSL with Ubuntu, VS Code with the Motoko and Remote WSL extensions, then Homebrew, Node 16 and dfx inside Ubuntu, and finally create, deploy and run the Hello project from a WSL VS Code window.</p>\n<h3>System requirements</h3>\n<ul><li>Windows 10 version 2004 or later (build 19041+) or Windows 11. Check under System Information.</li><li>64-bit Windows only (System type shows x64); 32-bit is unsupported.</li></ul>\n<h3>Install WSL (Windows Subsystem for Linux)</h3>\n<ul><li>Open PowerShell as administrator and run the wsl install command from the guide. It provides a Linux (Ubuntu) environment because the Internet Computer tooling needs bash.</li><li>Restart when told. On the next boot an Ubuntu window asks you to create a username and password; typed passwords are invisible. Pick something you will remember, since it is needed for sudo prompts in the next steps.</li><li>Back in an admin PowerShell, run the guide's verification command; Ubuntu with version 2 listed means WSL 2 is working.</li></ul>\n<h3>VS Code and extensions</h3>\n<ul><li>Install the stable VS Code with defaults, tick the desktop icon option.</li><li>Install the Motoko extension from the DFINITY Foundation (open the marketplace link and press Install so it launches VS Code).</li><li>Install the Remote WSL extension so VS Code can run its terminal and files inside Ubuntu.</li></ul>\n<h3>Tools inside WSL</h3>\n<ul><li>Node must be installed inside Ubuntu even if it already exists on Windows, because dfx runs in WSL.</li><li>Install Homebrew with the guide command (asks for the Ubuntu password and Enter to confirm). Complete the 'Next Steps' it prints: add Homebrew to PATH, and install the listed build dependencies (answer Y). When the prompt returns, brew --version confirms success.</li><li>brew install node@16 installs Node 16, the version that works with the IC tooling then. If brew reports node@16 is keg-only (not symlinked) because another Node exists, run brew link node@16. node --version should start with 16.</li><li>Run the dfx install command in Ubuntu. It prints where dfx was installed; that directory must be added to PATH. The guide gives a stub export line: replace the placeholder with the printed path, keep no space after the colon before the leading slash, paste it into Ubuntu, then use the guide's echo command to confirm the path was added. dfx --version should print 0.9.3.</li></ul>\n<h3>Create and run the project in WSL</h3>\n<ul><li>In VS Code use the green remote icon (bottom left) and choose New WSL Window; the window title/status must say WSL: Ubuntu.</li><li>In Ubuntu: mkdir ic-projects, cd into it, run dfx new hello (builds a sample app named hello; DFINITY logo shows when done). explorer.exe . opens the folder in Windows Explorer.</li><li>In the WSL VS Code window, Open Folder on ic-projects/hello. src contains main.mo (Motoko), plus HTML/JS frontend files. If main.mo has no highlighting, open the Extensions tab and press Install in WSL: Ubuntu on the Motoko extension.</li><li>Terminal &gt; New Terminal: dfx start; once it reports it is listening, split the terminal and run dfx deploy; then npm start and open the printed URL. Entering a name and pressing the button shows a greeting, confirming setup.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Windows PowerShell step</p><pre data-lang=\"sh\"># PowerShell (Run as administrator)\nwsl --install            # then restart; set Ubuntu username + password on first launch\nwsl -l -v                # (Extra) typical check; guide's command should show Ubuntu, VERSION 2</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Ubuntu/WSL steps (summary, placeholders for guide-specific commands)</p><pre data-lang=\"sh\"># Ubuntu (WSL) terminal\n# 1. Install Homebrew with the command from the guide, then run its 'Next Steps' PATH lines\n#    and install the Homebrew dependencies it lists\nbrew --version\nbrew install node@16\nbrew link node@16        # only if told node@16 is keg-only / not symlinked\nnode --version           # must start with 16\n# 2. Install dfx with the guide's command; note the install path it prints\nexport PATH=&quot;$PATH:&lt;INSTALL_PATH_FROM_DFX_INSTALLER&gt;&quot;   # no space after the colon; persist it in your shell profile\ndfx --version            # 0.9.3 in the video\n# 3. Project\nmkdir ic-projects &amp;&amp; cd ic-projects\ndfx new hello\nexplorer.exe .           # opens the folder in Windows Explorer\n# in VS Code opened as 'WSL: Ubuntu': terminal 1 -&gt; dfx start ; terminal 2 -&gt; dfx deploy ; npm start</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Reference repo frontend (newer scaffold): React component that calls the backend greet function</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import { useState } from 'react';\nimport { hello_backend } from 'declarations/hello_backend';\n\nfunction App() {\n  const [greeting, setGreeting] = useState('');\n\n  function handleSubmit(event) {\n    event.preventDefault();\n    const name = event.target.elements.name.value;\n    hello_backend.greet(name).then(setGreeting);   // call the canister, store its reply\n  }\n\n  return (\n    &lt;main&gt;\n      &lt;form onSubmit={handleSubmit}&gt;\n        &lt;label htmlFor=&quot;name&quot;&gt;Enter your name: &lt;/label&gt;\n        &lt;input id=&quot;name&quot; type=&quot;text&quot; /&gt;\n        &lt;button type=&quot;submit&quot;&gt;Click Me!&lt;/button&gt;\n      &lt;/form&gt;\n      &lt;section id=&quot;greeting&quot;&gt;{greeting}&lt;/section&gt;\n    &lt;/main&gt;\n  );\n}\nexport default App;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Reference repo backend (src/hello_backend/main.mo)</p><pre data-lang=\"mo\">persistent actor {\n  public query func greet(name : Text) : async Text {\n    return &quot;Hello, &quot; # name # &quot;!&quot;;   // # concatenates Text\n  };\n};</pre>\n<h2>The task: Windows setup: run Hello on the local Internet Computer</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Windows 10 2004+/11, 64-bit, admin rights.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> A working WSL Ubuntu environment where dfx start, dfx deploy and npm start serve the hello app and greet you.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Check Windows version (19041+) and x64.</li><li>Admin PowerShell: run wsl install, restart, set Ubuntu username/password, verify WSL version 2.</li><li>Install VS Code (desktop icon on), Motoko extension (DFINITY Foundation) and Remote WSL.</li><li>In Ubuntu install Homebrew, finish its Next Steps, check brew --version.</li><li>brew install node@16 (brew link node@16 if keg-only); node --version starts with 16.</li><li>Install dfx, add its printed install path to PATH, dfx --version.</li><li>VS Code: New WSL Window; in Ubuntu mkdir ic-projects, cd, dfx new hello.</li><li>Open ic-projects/hello in VS Code; enable the Motoko extension in WSL if highlighting is missing.</li><li>Terminal 1 dfx start; terminal 2 dfx deploy then npm start; open the URL and test the greeting.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Browser page with a name field and button that greets you using the backend canister.</p>\n</div>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Not running PowerShell as administrator for wsl install.</li><li>Forgetting to restart after installing WSL.</li><li>Using Windows-side Node instead of installing inside WSL.</li><li>Space after the colon in the PATH export line, or leaving the placeholder text.</li><li>Working in a non-WSL VS Code window (status bar must say WSL: Ubuntu).</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference repo hello project was made with a newer dfx scaffold (hello_backend/hello_frontend, Vite, persistent actor) than the 0.9.3-era folder structure in the video; commands and file names may differ from what you see on screen.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Current official guidance may no longer require Node 16 or Homebrew; check the DFINITY docs.</li></ul>");
